recently cryptocurrencies haven't had  the best of times bitcoin and ethereum  are down nearly 50  while many altcoins are down 70 to 80  and this makes sense given that interest  in bitcoin nfts and crypto in general  have fallen off substantially during  such downturns one of the most desirable  positions within the market is being the  person who's selling the shovels of  course crypto brokerages aren't making  nearly as much money as they used to  given lower trading volume and their own  stocks have been hammered by the market  coinbase for example is down nearly 70  percent from their all-time high  but despite this coinbase should still  be profitable meaning that they're able  to turn a profit in any sort of market  conditions  and as you would guess the guy behind  coinbase and brian armstrong is still  doing quite well with the net worth of  just over 5 billion dollars ironically  brian's net worth stood at 16 billion  just 12 months ago this means that his  average net worth over the past year is  about 10 billion dollars depending on  how the crypto markets are performing  coinbase however hasn't even been a  thing for 10 years at this point so  here's how brian armstrong made 10  billion dollars in 10 years  taking a look back brian's story dates  back to january 25th 1983 to san jose  california brian was born to a pretty  well-off family of engineers and from a  very young age he had a pretty strong  inclination towards technology nowadays  this is nothing special given that this  basically applies to all of us but back  in the 1980s and 90s average people were  just starting to buy their first  computers during high school it looks  like brian was the classic computer geek  who chose to learn java and css during  his free time brian recalls that getting  strange looks from his peers due to his  quote nerdiness was extremely common  weird looks weren't the only thing that  is coding skills got him though brian  was able to leverage his skills to  design websites and programs for local  businesses and earn some money on the  side soon enough ryan would graduate  from high school in 2001 and he would  attend rice university where he double  majored in computer science and  economics while he likely performed  great in school he never put all of his  eggs in one basket during college he  opened up a website that could match  tutors with students called  universitytutor.com the service wasn't  anything particularly special but brian  was rather early to the game having  launched to serve this in june of 2003  so he was able to capture quite a bit of  market share and this would turn into a  full-time job over the next several  years brian didn't drop out of college  either though  instead he would actually go on to  complete a master's in computer science  at rice university right after he  finishes bachelor's brian also got an  internship at ibm where he helped them  create some java based tools that could  be used in conjunction with the network  attached storage devices  anyway shortly after finishing college  bryan would decide to take a one-year  trip to buenos aires argentina and this  trip would turn out to be extremely  influential you see argentina has been  struggling with a chronic inflation  problem for decades in 2004 you could  only get 2.81 argentine pesos with one  dollar  today however you can get  114.39 argentine pesos meaning that the  currency is worth 40 times less relative  to the dollar  living in buenos aires brian was  witnessing this crisis firsthand and  this didn't give him a particularly  positive view towards fiat currency  after brian returned to the us he  continued serving as the ceo of  universitytutor.com  but i don't think you'd be surprised to  hear that brian was also constantly on  the lookout for new opportunities one of  these opportunities was working at  airbnb as a software engineer  at the time airbnb was operating in 190  countries and brian had a chance to work  on their international payment systems  this experience made him realize just  how difficult it was to send and receive  money in certain developing countries  and this would give him the final push  he needed to go all in on coinbase  while it wasn't till mid 2012 that brian  left university tutor and went all in on  coinbase by this point he had already  been working on the idea for a few years  in the background brian first heard  about bitcoin in december of 2010 when  he came across bitcoin's white paper  being a serial entrepreneur since high  school brian instantly knew that he had  to try something in this space but he  wasn't sure what exactly to do initially  it seems like brian was interested in  creating something like venmo or square  but for bitcoin however he quickly  realized that bitcoin wasn't that great  when it came to transactions due to its  high fees so instead he decided to just  focus on helping people buy and hold  crypto and this of course led him down  the path of starting a crypto brokerage  over the next two years brian would  spend all of his free time writing code  that would allow users to buy and store  various cryptocurrencies brian also  spent a lot of his time on the bitcoin  subreddit to better understand what  crypto enthusiasts were looking for and  this is when brian would run into a man  named fred ursum fred had a classic wall  street job as a foreign exchange trader  at goldman sachs in his free time he  liked to trade bitcoin and this is what  led him to the bitcoin subreddit as you  might have guessed brian and fred would  quickly become friends and fred would  join coinbase as a co-founder the duo  was also able to secure 150 000 worth of  startup capital from y combinator which  helped tremendously with initial startup  expenses and with that the duo launched  coinbase on june 20th 2012. at the time  coinbase was one of the only choices you  had if you wanted to conveniently buy  bitcoin within the us so naturally all  of the original crypto enthusiasts  flocked over to coinbase kraken and  bitstamp for many of these guys the  brokerage didn't matter all that much  given that they had no plans of keeping  their coins on the brokerage most of  these guys instantly transfer their  coins into their bitcoin wallets and  eventually into cold storage options  nonetheless coinbase was able to make  quite a bit of money with each bitcoin  purchase or sale thanks to their hefty  commission fees  even today their fees are rather high  especially in comparison to the stock  market for example if you buy 10 000  worth of crypto you have to pay coinbase  between 40 and 60  despite these fees coinbase has never  really struggled with a lack of interest  given that crypto returns are so high  that these fees often seem insignificant  in comparison within just the first two  years of launching coinbase was able to  grow to 1 million users and this  exponential growth made it extremely  easy to raise money in may of 2013 union  square ventures invested 5 million  dollars into coinbase at the time this  seemed like an extremely risky move  given that this was the biggest funding  run in bitcoin startup history but  knowing that the stake would eventually  grow to 4.6 billion dollars i think it  worked out just fine  just a couple of months after this when  base would raise another 25 million  dollars and with that they were off to  the races  using all this newfound capital brian  would continue making coinbase more  secure for example in 2014 brian  purchased entrants for all of the  bitcoin stored on coinbase servers  similarly coinbase also launched their  vault system which is supposed to offer  extremely secure bitcoin storage such  initiatives made coinbase customers feel  a lot more comfortable and many of them  started to hold their crypto inside  coinbase itself  aside from upping the reliability of  coinbase brian also worked on expanding  their features one of their biggest  launches came in 2015 when the company  came out with coinbase exchange this  exchange was more geared towards crypto  traders and enthusiasts and boasted  significant lower fees and smaller bid  ask spreads it also came along with more  sophisticated charts indicators and  limit orders yet i don't think coinbase  exchange was ever able to reach its full  potential first of all they had a  branding crisis for a few years  initially they were renamed a global  digital asset exchange before they were  eventually renamed coinbase pro  aside from this branding issue i don't  think coinbase pro actually ever reached  its target market even though coinbase  pro is an exceptional service and quite  popular the vast majority of people who  use coinbase pro are simply crypto  hodlers who want lower fees  it seems like the high frequency traders  far prefer the offerings from binance  which is evident in their substantially  higher trading volume coinbase currently  averages about 1 billion dollars per day  in trading volume meanwhile finance  averages nearly 30 billion per day in  trading volume clearly while coinbase  did not quite win over the trading  market this experience allowed them to  better understand their average customer  and position in the market coinbase  isn't the platform for traders it's the  platform for everyday crypto holders and  this became extremely evident during the  2017 bull run in the last quarter of  2017 coinbase's user count nearly  doubled in a matter of months we saw a  similar growth multiple in the first  quarter of 2021 as well brian has since  chosen to lean into the casual  demographic and launch features that  appeal to them for example in october of  2020 coinbase worked with visa to launch  their own debit card which apparently  offers up to four percent back in  rewards and more recently coinbase ran  an extremely successful ad at the 2022  super bowl the ad wasn't anything super  flashy or anything it was literally just  a qr code that was bouncing off the  walls of the screen however given that  it garnered 20 million hits in one  minute crashed into our coinbase website  and it caused coinbase to jump from 186  in the app store to number two i think  it's safe to say that the ad was a  roaring success and that brings us to  where we are today  likely the biggest event for coinbase in  recent history aside from the super bowl  added is their ipo  unfortunately though unlike the super  bowl added their ipo was not a roaring  success the ipo was actually extremely  hyped and people bought in significantly  on the day of the ipo but coinbase has  sold off ever since  also something that we should note is  that the peak of bitcoin was literally  on the exact day that coinbase ipo'd  bitcoin did go on to make a new all-time  high last november but this new peak was  only marginally higher than the old peak  and i think the general consensus is  that the april peak was a much more  significant peak so coinbase's ipo was  literally the peak of the crypto market  this is quite ironic given that coinbase  usually has the opposite effect on  crypto whenever a crypto is listed on  coinbase it often sources given that a  lot more people are able to buy the  given crypto but i guess coinbase  themselves being listed was bad luck for  the crypto market at least in the short  term looking forward given that crypto  is still only in its infancy it's likely  that coinbase has significant growth  left both in terms of their total user  count and their average holdings per  user and at this rate i think that brian  is on track to hit the 100 billion mark  within the next 10 to 20 years if not  sooner and fortunately brian is trying  his best to put all this wealth to good  use a few years ago he founded  givecrypto.com which is a non-profit  that distributes cryptocurrency to  people living in poverty he has also  assigned the giving pledge which means  that he has pledged to give away the  vast majority of his wealth considering  these wholesome ambitions i believe he  deserves all the success he's achieved  so far but that's just what i think  who's your favorite crypto brokerage  comment that down below  also drop a like if you're a fan of  crypto and of course consider checking  out our international channels to watch  our videos in other languages and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  you
 

